# RoadLife RP – City Rulebook

Welcome to RoadLife RP. This city is built on realistic roleplay, respect and fair play for everyone. Whether you are a member of the public, a criminal, in a gang, a police officer or hospital staff, these rules apply to you.

Nobody is above the rules. Your job, gang, rank, how long you have played or who your mates are does not let you ignore them.

**Our golden rule: roleplay to create a story, not to win a scene.**

---

## 1. General rules

These apply to everyone in the city.

### 1.1 Stay in character
- Stay in character while you are in the city.
- Don't talk about out-of-city matters during active roleplay.
- Don't break character because a scene isn't going your way.
- If you have a problem with a scene, finish it where you can and contact staff afterwards.
- Only staff can call an out-of-character chat in the middle of a scene.

### 1.2 Respect everyone
- Respect every player, whatever their job, gang or friendships.
- Bullying, harassment, discrimination and personal attacks are not allowed. No racism, sexism or hate speech.
- An in-character falling-out never gives you the right to have a go at someone out of character.
- Leave real-life arguments out of the city.

### 1.3 No metagaming
- Don't use information from outside the city to help your character.
- Discord messages, streams, clips, screenshots and private chats are not in-character information.
- Your character can only act on what they have actually found out in the city.

### 1.4 No powergaming
- Don't force actions or outcomes on another player.
- Give people a fair chance to respond to what you do.
- Don't use game mechanics to get unrealistic results.

### 1.5 No random deathmatching (RDM)
- You can't attack or kill another player without a proper roleplay reason.
- Violence needs proper roleplay build-up first.
- Shooting people because you don't like them is not allowed.

### 1.6 No vehicle deathmatching (VDM)
- Don't use vehicles as weapons without a proper roleplay reason.
- Deliberately running people over is not allowed.
- Don't keep ramming cars just to ruin someone's night.

### 1.7 Value your life
- Your character must value their life.
- If a gun is pointed at you and you are genuinely outnumbered or caught out, react realistically and comply.
- Don't act fearless in a life-or-death moment without a believable reason.

### 1.8 No combat logging
- Don't log off during an active scene to avoid what happens next.
- If your game crashes, come back as soon as you can and let the players involved or staff know.
- You can't switch characters to get out of an ongoing scene.

### 1.9 Cheating and exploits
- Cheats, mod menus, exploits and any third-party advantage are banned.
- Duplication glitches and abusing bugs for gain are banned.
- Report any exploit you find to staff.
- Don't accuse people of cheating without evidence. Use the proper report process.

### 1.10 Injuries
- Roleplay your injuries realistically.
- Being shot, stabbed, in a bad crash or falling from height has consequences.
- You can't walk off serious injuries as if nothing happened.

### 1.11 Menu Protection and Player Interaction

Players must not exploit another player's inability to respond while they are interacting with an in-game menu.

- You cannot rob, kidnap, take hostage, attack or kill someone while they are actively using a clothing, barber, tattoo, food ordering, mechanic or vehicle customisation menu.
- You cannot initiate hostile roleplay against someone who is unable to see, move or respond properly because of a menu.
- You must allow the player a reasonable opportunity to exit the menu and participate in the roleplay before initiating hostile actions.
- Players cannot deliberately enter or remain in menus to avoid an ongoing robbery, kidnapping, confrontation or other hostile roleplay.
- Menu protection does not cancel an already active roleplay scene. Players must not exploit menus to escape consequences.

**The principle is simple: both sides must have a fair opportunity to participate in the roleplay.**

---

## 2. Rules for members of the public

Not being in a gang or a job doesn't mean the roleplay rules don't apply.

### 2.1 Conduct
- Build a realistic, believable character.
- Don't keep throwing yourself into criminal scenes just to cause trouble.
- Don't get involved in police, gang or other ongoing scenes without a good reason.
- Respect the emergency services and let them do their job.

### 2.2 Robberies
- Robberies need a proper roleplay reason.
- No robbing people at random with no interaction.
- Give the victim a fair chance to take part in the scene.
- Don't keep picking on the same person because they are an easy target.

### 2.3 Kidnapping and hostages
- Kidnappings need proper roleplay build-up.
- Hostages must be genuine participants.
- No using friends or gang members as fake hostages to play the police.
- Hostage scenes must be handled realistically.

### 2.4 Dealing with the police
- Don't wind up officers just to start a chase or a shootout.
- If you are pulled over, take part in the stop properly.
- Don't turn every vehicle stop into violence.
- You can argue with the police in character, but never break character to do it.

### 2.5 Scams and theft
- Any scam or theft must follow the city's criminal rules.
- No using game mechanics or loopholes to steal money or items.
- If you accuse someone of theft, back it up with proper roleplay and evidence.

---

## 3. Gang and criminal rules

Gangs are here for organised crime, rivalries and real stories, not endless shooting.

### 3.1 Respect other gangs
- Every gang must respect that other crews exist.
- Wearing your colours doesn't give you the right to attack anyone.
- No shooting another gang's members at random without a proper reason.
- Disputes should build through roleplay, not jump straight to a shootout.

### 3.2 Starting hostile roleplay
- Hostile scenes need clear initiation, unless staff have approved a gang war that says otherwise.
- The other side must clearly understand the situation has turned hostile.
- Don't use a small disagreement as an excuse for a mass shooting.
- Once it kicks off, everyone involved keeps roleplaying properly.

### 3.3 Gang wars
- Gang wars need a real in-character reason.
- A personal falling-out between two people doesn't drag in whole gangs.
- Gang wars may need staff approval.
- Alliances and extra members must stay within the city's gang limits.
- Organised conflicts follow any event rules management sets.

### 3.4 Gang numbers
- Stick to the city's limits for robberies, shootouts and organised crime.
- No using unofficial allies or extra people to get round the limits.
- Anyone giving backup or passing on tactical info counts as taking part.

### 3.5 Robbing other players
- Have proper roleplay before you rob someone.
- Don't keep robbing the same person without real roleplay.
- Don't strip items with no in-character value just to empty someone's pockets.
- You can't rob someone only because they are in a rival gang.

### 3.6 Gang turf
- Respect other gangs' turf.
- Being on someone else's turf is not a reason to kill them.
- Use intimidation, conversation and existing rivalries to build the scene.
- Constant shooting on turf with no real roleplay is not allowed.

### 3.7 Hospital and recovery after a gang fight
This one matters a lot.
- If you are killed in a gang fight and wake up in hospital, you are injured and vulnerable.
- You can't walk straight out, grab your guns and go back after the people who killed you.
- No revenge straight after treatment or a respawn.
- Roleplay the physical and mental effects of your injuries.
- Don't use anything about your death that your character wouldn't remember.
- Don't tell your gang where your killers are while you are down.
- You can't rejoin the same shootout after being revived or respawning.
- Being revived is not the same as being fully recovered.

### 3.8 No revenge killing
- Don't come back to kill someone just because they killed you.
- Don't use a hospital respawn to reset a fight.
- Any future payback must come from new, genuine roleplay.

---

## 4. Police rules

This covers Police, Rural Police and Motorway Police. Wearing the uniform comes with responsibility, maturity and being able to handle tough scenes.

### 4.1 Stay in character, always
One of the most important rules in the city.
- Stay in character through every interaction.
- Don't break character because a suspect gets away, won't cooperate, challenges you or comes out on top.
- Don't get angry out of character because a scene didn't go how you wanted.
- Not every arrest, chase or investigation ends with a police win. Accept that.
- Don't use your powers to force an outcome because you disagree with someone's roleplay.
- If it doesn't go your way, carry on in character and deal with it.

**You are a police officer, not a player who has to win every scene.**

### 4.2 Respond to the city, not to people
- Prioritise real calls: shots fired, robberies, crashes and 999 calls.
- Don't drive round looking for particular players to stop or follow.
- Once a scene is finished and suspects are dealt with, don't go straight back to chasing the same people. Write your reports and move on.
- Don't keep stopping or searching the same person without a fresh, genuine reason.

### 4.3 Evidence and accusations
- You need reasonable in-character grounds for any accusation, and you must treat evidence fairly.
- You can't accuse someone of theft just because you think it was them.
- If you search someone and don't find the stolen property, that is not proof they cheated.
- With no stolen items and no other credible evidence, the accusation is not proven.
- Never make up evidence to justify an arrest.
- Never accuse someone of cheating because they deny it or because your investigation came up empty.
- If there is independent evidence, like credible witnesses or CCTV, you can carry on a proper investigation in character.
- Real suspicion of cheating goes to staff with evidence, never argued out in the scene.

**A search that finds nothing is not proof of cheating. Suspicion is not a conviction.**

### 4.4 Fair arrests and searches
- Arrests need genuine in-character grounds.
- Searches follow police procedure.
- Don't search people just because you don't like them.
- Never plant evidence.
- Explain why someone is being arrested and let them take part in the roleplay.
- Never use police powers to settle a personal grudge.

### 4.5 Professional behaviour
- Speak respectfully to members of the public and to criminals.
- Don't use the badge to throw your weight around.
- Stay calm in confrontations.
- Don't insult, bait or wind up suspects.
- Keep personal disagreements out of police work.

### 4.6 Pursuits
- Handle pursuits realistically and think about public safety.
- Don't ram vehicles without a proper reason.
- A chase is not an excuse for unnecessary force.
- Give suspects a fair chance to take part in the pursuit.

### 4.7 Use of force
- Force must match the threat.
- Don't draw or fire weapons unnecessarily.
- You can't shoot someone just for refusing to cooperate verbally.
- Know the difference between a real threat and someone being awkward.
- Excessive or revenge force is banned.

### 4.8 Corruption
- No corruption without management approval.
- Never abuse police information, kit, weapons or evidence for personal gain.
- Never leak police information to gangs or friends.
- Never use your position to protect friends from a genuine investigation.

### 4.9 Responsibility
- Take part properly in investigations, arrests, interviews and court roleplay where it's available.
- Don't walk away from a scene because it becomes inconvenient.
- Work professionally with the hospital and other services.
- Senior ranks are expected to set the example.

### 4.10 Complaints
- Never stop a live scene to argue about a rule.
- If you think someone broke a rule, collect evidence and open a staff ticket.
- Officers are held to the same standards as everyone else. The uniform doesn't let you break character.

---

## 5. Hospital and paramedic rules

Hospital staff and paramedics bring medical roleplay to the city and help keep it realistic.

### 5.1 Professional conduct
- Stay in character while on duty.
- Treat every patient professionally, whatever their gang, record or past with you.
- Don't refuse treatment because you dislike someone.

### 5.2 Medical roleplay
- Assess and treat injuries realistically.
- Ask how the injuries happened.
- Patients must go along with the medical roleplay.
- Don't rush treatment just to get someone back out.

### 5.3 Recovery
- Patients get a fair amount of time to recover.
- Being discharged doesn't mean you are fit to fight.
- Give sensible recovery advice, and patients must respect it.

### 5.4 The hospital is neutral
- The hospital is neutral ground.
- Staff can't favour one gang over another.
- No passing weapons, drugs or secret information through the hospital.
- Staff must not help criminals escape police custody.
- No carrying on gang disputes on hospital grounds.

### 5.5 Safety of medical staff
- Don't target or kidnap hospital staff or paramedics, unless it is an exceptional, properly built scene with management approval.
- Let medical staff work without interference.
- Medical staff must not use their role to give friends or their gang an unfair edge.

### 5.6 Integrity
- Never fake medical records.
- Never misuse or sell medical equipment.
- Never revive someone just to help them win a fight.
- Keep patient information private unless there is a genuine in-character reason.

---

## 6. Death and hospital rules

These apply to everyone, whatever your gang or job.

### 6.1 What happens when you die
- If another player kills you, what you were carrying drops in a grave where you died: items, guns, cash and dirty money. 30% of the cash and dirty money is lost.
- Your XP is never touched, and your bank is always safe, so keep your money in the bank.
- Dying to NPCs, crashing or falling costs you nothing.
- After a hospital respawn you stay in the bed for 2 minutes to recover.
- New players get 2 hours of protection: nobody can hurt you and you can't hurt anyone. Joining a gang ends it. Type /protection to check.

### 6.2 New life rule (NLR)
- After a hospital respawn you forget what led up to your death: who killed you, where and how, unless you find out again through roleplay.
- Stay at least 150 metres away from where you died for 15 minutes.
- No guns for 10 minutes after you respawn.
- Don't use friends, Discord or anything outside the game to get back what your character forgot.

### 6.3 While you are down
- No talking on voice or radio to pass on tactical information.
- No calling your gang to set up revenge.
- Don't move around unrealistically while injured.
- Wait for medical help or use the respawn.

### 6.4 After being revived
- You can't jump straight back into a shootout.
- No revenge on whoever hurt you.
- Roleplay your injuries. Being revived doesn't wipe away what happened.

### 6.5 No fighting at the hospital
- No shooting, fighting or robbing inside the hospital.
- Don't use the hospital entrance to ambush people.
- Don't wait outside the hospital to kill people who have just respawned.
- Let hospital staff do their jobs.

---

## 7. Business and legal job rules

### 7.1 Businesses
- Run your business fairly.
- Don't abuse business permissions to give mates an advantage.
- Don't exploit business money or stock.
- Businesses should create roleplay for the public.

### 7.2 Legal jobs
- Do legal jobs properly.
- Don't use job kit or vehicles for crime without approved roleplay.
- Don't exploit job mechanics to make money unfairly.
- Council and public-service workers must act professionally.

### 7.3 Mechanics
- Charge fair prices in line with the city's prices.
- Never damage cars on purpose or sabotage repairs.
- Don't use the job to give criminals an unfair helping hand.
- Keep customer interactions in character.

### 7.4 Money and items
- No real-money trading of in-game items, cars or money.
- Don't exploit bugs for money. Report them to staff instead.

---

## 8. Staff and community

### 8.1 Staff
- Staff enforce the rules fairly.
- Staff never favour friends, gangs or services.
- Decisions are based on evidence and the full situation.
- Staff don't use their position to interfere with genuine roleplay.

### 8.2 Reporting a rule break
- Don't argue about a rule break in the middle of a scene.
- Collect clips, screenshots or other evidence.
- Open a ticket in our Discord: discord.gg/roadliferp
- Don't stir up harassment or public arguments against another player.

### 8.3 No retaliation
- Never target someone because they reported you.
- Staff reports stay separate from in-character disputes.
- Reporting a genuine concern is never an excuse for others to harass you.

---

## Punishments

Breaking the rules can lead to action. Depending on how serious it is, how often it happens and the situation, this can be:

- A verbal warning
- An official warning
- Being removed from the city for a while
- A temporary ban
- Dismissal from your service, or action within your gang
- A permanent ban

Serious offences, including cheating, exploiting, harassment and deliberately wrecking roleplay, can mean removal straight away. Repeated rule breaking leads to tougher action each time. Staff can look into any incident and will consider the context, the evidence and past behaviour before deciding.

---

## The RoadLife standard

We want a city where:
- People can enjoy their lives without being targeted all the time.
- Gangs build a name through real roleplay, not endless shooting.
- Police understand their job is to create realistic policing roleplay, not to win every scene.
- Hospital staff can give medical roleplay without being treated as a revive service.
- Criminals understand that death and injury have consequences.
- Everyone understands that losing a scene is part of roleplay.

## A final word to everyone in the city

You won't win every argument. You won't win every shootout. You won't escape every arrest. You won't always get the outcome you wanted.

That's roleplay.

The best roleplayers aren't the ones who win every time. They're the ones who can take a loss, carry on their story and make it fun for everyone involved.

Respect the city. Respect the roleplay. Respect each other.

**Welcome to RoadLife RP.**
